#!/usr/bin/env bpftrace
// ZEPH eBPF sensor — DNS query metadata observation (Phase 5 spec section
// 10). Hook: uprobe on libc's getaddrinfo() — reads the hostname argument
// (arg0, a plain C string) the calling process ALREADY resolved into its
// own memory before calling the function; this is metadata already present
// in-process, not packet/payload inspection (spec section 3's own
// boundary — the DNS wire protocol itself is never touched, no port-53
// packet is parsed). This is the well-documented "watch what a process
// asks the resolver for" technique — the DNS-equivalent of Phase 4's own
// sched_process_exec tracepoint watching what a process execs.
//
// Deliberately does NOT hook the matching uretprobe to read the resolved
// IP/NXDOMAIN status — the return value of getaddrinfo() is an integer
// error code (0 = success, EAI_NONAME = NXDOMAIN, etc.), which IS also
// pure metadata and a natural future addition, but is left out of this
// pass to keep the hook surface minimal and because nxdomain detection
// this way needs care around getaddrinfo's own retry/fallback behavior
// across libc versions — see the final report's honest scope note. The
// dns field's nxdomain flag ebpf-sensor/src/dns.js sets is therefore
// always absent from THIS script's own output; it exists in the schema for
// a future pass to populate.
//
// Path is HARDCODED to the standard glibc location on Debian/Ubuntu (ZEPH's
// actual target distros — see infra/serv00.md, infra/render.md). bpftrace
// resolves a uprobe's target path at PARSE time, not runtime, so it cannot
// be templated from a CLI argument or env var the way scripts/network.bt's
// numeric fields are decoded in Node instead — this is a real bpftrace
// constraint, not a shortcut. A host with libc somewhere else (musl/
// Alpine, a non-standard install) needs this ONE line edited directly;
// config.js's NETWORK_SENSOR_LIBC_PATH exists to document the intent and
// is read by a future templating pass if this becomes a real deployment
// need, but is NOT currently wired into this file — see
// ebpf-sensor/README.md's own note on this exact limitation.

uprobe:/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6:getaddrinfo
{
    printf("{\"kind\":\"dns\",\"pid\":%d,\"comm\":\"%s\",\"domain\":\"%s\",\"ts\":%llu}\n",
        pid, comm, str(arg0), nsecs);
}
